#!/bin/sh
# Stops a retired personal name from re-entering the tree.
#
# Why a hook and not a line in a contributing doc: a naming pass is a moment,
# a hook is a guarantee. People and agents forget; this does not.
#
# NOTE ON THE PATTERN. It is a regex, and the bracket is not decoration: it
# lets this file match the name without ever spelling it contiguously. A
# committed file that spells a retired name out is itself a place the name
# lives -- which is the thing being prevented. Do not "tidy" the brackets away.
#
# THE ALLOWANCE IS DELIBERATE. The intro and Origin sections of README.md and
# the header of CLAUDE.md record who authored a real, released 2013 product.
# That is factual provenance: re-crediting it would make the record false,
# deleting it would make it incomplete. Those lines stay, and each carries a
# note explaining why. If they are ever removed, set these to 0 and this hook
# becomes an absolute block.
#
# Counts are LINES containing a match, not occurrences within a line.

PATTERN='chlo[e]'

allowance() {
  case "$1" in
    CLAUDE.md) echo 1 ;;
    README.md) echo 2 ;;
    *)         echo 0 ;;
  esac
}

fail=0

for f in $(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM); do
  allow=$(allowance "$f")
  # Staged content, not the worktree -- what is about to be committed.
  # -I skips binaries so a stray image cannot trip this.
  n=$(git show ":$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -Eic "$PATTERN")
  [ -z "$n" ] && n=0

  if [ "$n" -gt "$allow" ]; then
    echo "  BLOCKED  $f -- $n line(s) carry a retired name, $allow allowed"
    git show ":$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -Ein "$PATTERN" | sed 's/^/           /'
    fail=1
  fi
done

if [ "$fail" -ne 0 ]; then
  cat <<'EOF'

Commit stopped: a retired personal name would enter the tree.

Credits, author fields, footers and comments go to SACRVM, not to a person.
State a decision and its reason rather than who made it -- that reads shorter
than the attribution anyway.

If you are genuinely adding factual provenance about a real released product,
raise the allowance for that file in .githooks/pre-commit and leave a note at
the line itself, so the next reader does not "fix" a true record.

Override once (you almost never should):  git commit --no-verify
EOF
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
